Author: vincent <vincent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Mon Oct 26 07:17:45 2026 +1000

    zcode: a ZCode whose files were rewritten is put back by a sync (#3246)
    
    ZCode's Sync gave up as soon as magpie's provider was in neither of its
    files: it returned before writing anything. ZCode rewrites its own config on
    a restart or an update, and its settings page edits provider_config.json
    alone — so magpie's provider gone from both files was something outside
    magpie that rewrote them, and the connection stayed lost until the user
    connected it again (the reporter saw the files' mtimes move only on a manual
    connect, while the gateway kept running).
    
    A sync now puts magpie's provider back into both files when magpie applied it
    (applied.json) and the user didn't disconnect it here. One taken out of
    provider_config.json in ZCode's settings stays out, as before, and so does
    the removal from an older ZCode that keeps its providers in config.json
    alone.
    
    RED->GREEN: TestZCodeSyncRestoresARewrite (internal/agent) connects magpie's
    provider at path as the editor does (Agent.Apply, which records it), has
    ZCode rewrite both files without it, then runs a sync. At 01468003 the
    provider stays gone ("after a rewrite: \"\", want magpie back"); with this
    change both files carry it again with ZCode's own rules kept, and a disconnect
    through magpie is not undone by the same rewrite.
    
    Ran: go test -tags nogui ./internal/agent (ok), gofmt -l on the changed files
    (empty), go vet ./..., GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go vet -tags nogui ./...,
    GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./..., go build ./... and the linux nogui and
    windows builds — all ok. Not tried: ZCode 3.16.2 itself, which isn't
    installed here; the rewrite is simulated from the reporter's evidence.

